    {
      "id": "title",
      "kind": "section",
      "purpose": "Opening: title, tagline and hero.",
      "html": "<header class=\"t\"><p class=\"kicker\">2025, six weeks</p>\n<h1>Maré Cold Brew</h1><p class=\"tag\">A launch landing page for a small cold brew brand from Santos.</p></header>\n<figure class=\"hero\"><img src=\"{{evidence:hero}}\" alt=\"Maré landing page hero with a cold brew bottle on a wave\"></figure>",
      "css": ".t{padding:140px 120px 80px}\n.kicker{font:500 20px var(--pd-font-body);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--pd-muted)}\nh1{font:600 104px/1.02 var(--pd-font-heading);margin:24px 0}\n.tag{font:400 32px/1.4 var(--pd-font-body);max-width:900px}\n.hero{padding:0 120px 140px}.hero img{width:100%;border-radius:12px;box-shadow:0 30px 80px rgba(0,0,0,.15)}",
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "context",
      "kind": "section",
      "purpose": "The challenge",
      "html": "<section class=\"s\"><h2>The challenge</h2><div class=\"b\"><p>Maré is a two person coffee brand from the São Paulo coast, selling bottled cold brew online and through a handful of cafés to young professionals.</p><p>Orders lived in Instagram DMs. Reorders got lost, subscriptions were impossible to explain, and the founders were answering messages at midnight. The goal: one page that explains the product, sells the subscription and takes orders, on a tiny budget and a builder the founders can edit themselves.</p></div></section>",
      "css": ".s{padding:140px 120px;display:grid;grid-template-columns:360px 1fr;gap:80px}\nh2{font:600 44px/1.1 var(--pd-font-heading);color:var(--pd-accent)}\n.b p{font:400 28px/1.55 var(--pd-font-body);margin-bottom:28px;max-width:820px}",
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "role",
      "kind": "section",
      "purpose": "My role",
      "html": "<section class=\"s\"><h2>My role</h2><div class=\"b\"><p>Freelance designer. I ran customer conversations, structured the copy, designed the page and built it in the no-code tool. The founders provided content and product photos.</p></div></section>",
      "css": ".s{padding:140px 120px;display:grid;grid-template-columns:360px 1fr;gap:80px}\nh2{font:600 44px/1.1 var(--pd-font-heading);color:var(--pd-accent)}\n.b p{font:400 28px/1.55 var(--pd-font-body);margin-bottom:28px;max-width:820px}",
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "process",
      "kind": "section",
      "purpose": "Process",
      "html": "<section class=\"s\"><h2>Process</h2><div class=\"b\"><p>Three months of DMs held the brief: the same five questions, over and over. After six customer conversations, the page was written as plain text answering those questions in order, then wireframed, then designed. The subscription became the default choice. A recipe blog was cut: nice, but nobody asked for it.</p></div></section>",
      "css": ".s{padding:140px 120px;display:grid;grid-template-columns:360px 1fr;gap:80px}\nh2{font:600 44px/1.1 var(--pd-font-heading);color:var(--pd-accent)}\n.b p{font:400 28px/1.55 var(--pd-font-body);margin-bottom:28px;max-width:820px}",
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "solution",
      "kind": "section",
      "purpose": "The solution",
      "html": "<section class=\"s\"><h2>The solution</h2><div class=\"b\"><p>One landing page: a hero with the bottle riding a wave, a section answering the five real customer questions, a bottle size picker, a subscription toggle set on by default, and a delivery map for the coast. It reads like the founders talking, not like a coffee ad.</p></div></section>",
      "css": ".s{padding:140px 120px;display:grid;grid-template-columns:360px 1fr;gap:80px}\nh2{font:600 44px/1.1 var(--pd-font-heading);color:var(--pd-accent)}\n.b p{font:400 28px/1.55 var(--pd-font-body);margin-bottom:28px;max-width:820px}",
      "locked": false
    },
